Postpartum Haemorrhage
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Excessive postpartum bleeding, Boggy poorly contracted uterus, Hemodynamic instability

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, massive vaginal bleeding passing large clots causing severe presyncope 5 days post-vaginal delivery.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden onset of catastrophic vaginal hemorrhage (soaking a maternity pad every 15 minutes) and impending hypovolemic shock (dizziness, pallor) in a 5-day postpartum patient. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately initiate bimanual uterine compression, administer pharmacological uterotonics, and coordinate emergency priority 1 ambulance transport to save the patient's life before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had my baby 5 days ago, and suddenly blood is gushing out of me, I am passing clots the size of oranges, and I can't stay awake.
History of Present Illness: The patient, 5 days post-uncomplicated vaginal delivery, reports a sudden, massive increase in vaginal bleeding exceeding normal lochia. She describes a continuous large gush of bright red blood from the vagina, soaking through heavy maternity pads in under 15 minutes. She reports the passage of multiple large, organized blood clots. She complains of severe dizziness upon attempting to stand, profound generalized weakness, and blurred vision, indicating acute hemodynamic instability. She denies fever or foul-smelling discharge.
Functional Status: Supine on the floor; unable to safely sit or stand due to severe presyncope and weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent life-threatening hemorrhagic shock.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 mmHg | HR: 135 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly pale, diaphoretic, lethargic, and exhibiting altered mental status (confusion/drowsiness).
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, boggy, and poorly contracted uterus palpable well above the umbilicus. Visual inspection of the perineum confirms active, continuous, heavy bright red bleeding with large clots present. The perineum is intact without obvious external lacerations to account for the volume. Signs of impending hypovolemic shock are evident (tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, delayed capillary refill >4 seconds).
Environmental Observations: Floor and clothing heavily saturated with fresh blood.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Secondary Postpartum Haemorrhage (Severe Uterine Atony vs. Retained Products) - Status: Hemorrhagic Shock
Differentials:

Uterine Atony - The most likely primary cause given the palpably boggy, high uterus, though secondary PPH (after 24h) often involves retained products preventing contraction.

Retained Placental Fragments - Highly suspicious in secondary PPH; pieces of placenta prevent the uterus from clamping down, leading to massive bleeding. Requires surgical exploration (D&C).

Vaginal/Cervical Laceration - Unlikely to be the sole cause of this massive, sudden gush on day 5, and the boggy uterus points firmly to an intrauterine source.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Critical threat to life)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in acute hypovolemic shock secondary to a massive secondary postpartum hemorrhage. The diagnostic tetrad of excessive bleeding, a soft boggy uterus, and profound hemodynamic instability is clearly present. Immediate, aggressive after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to perform life-saving physical maneuvers (bimanual massage) to mechanically compress the bleeding vessels, administer available uterotonics, and manage the airway/circulation while emergency services (000) respond for priority transport and massive transfusion protocols. This is an absolute "load and go" emergency that could not wait.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for "Priority 1 - Severe Postpartum Hemorrhage / Shock". Placed the patient perfectly flat with legs elevated. Commenced immediate, continuous, aggressive fundal massage to physically force the uterus to contract and expel clots. Administered intramuscular Oxytocin 10 units (from GP emergency bag) and oral Tranexamic Acid 1g to promote clotting. Secured a large-bore (14G) IV cannula while awaiting paramedics.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain maximum blood flow to your brain and heart, you must remain absolutely flat on the floor and not attempt to lift your head; recognizing that this keeps you conscious will enforce strict compliance. To assist the paramedics, allow them to manage all of your movement onto the stretcher; understanding that any physical effort increases bleeding will ensure you remain completely passive.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover of a critical patient provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently under direct medical supervision awaiting immediate ambulance transport for life-saving resuscitation and potential emergency surgery.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a critical, life-threatening emergency requiring immediate hospital resuscitation and likely surgery to stop the bleeding.
Clinical Course: The ambulance will take you directly to the resuscitation bay. You will receive intravenous fluids and blood transfusions immediately upon arrival.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to move, sit up, or speak unnecessarily. Keep your legs elevated and wait for the paramedics.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your womb has failed to clamp down after birth, likely because a small piece of the placenta was left behind. This is allowing the blood vessels to bleed freely, causing a massive hemorrhage. I am physically massaging your womb to force it to clamp shut, and I have given you an injection to help it contract, but you are in shock from blood loss. You must go to the hospital immediately by ambulance to replace the lost blood and have the womb cleaned out surgically.
Actionable Steps: Lie completely still. Let your mother care for the baby. Focus on taking slow, shallow breaths while we wait for the ambulance.

